<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Human Rights Act 1998</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Human Rights Act 1998 is a law of the United Kingdom that incorporated the rights of the European Convention on Human Rights into domestic law. In force from October 2000, it allows people to bring rights cases in British courts and requires public bodies to respect Convention rights, while leaving Parliament the final word on legislation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/human-rights-act-1998.jpg" alt="Human Rights Act 1998" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_coat_of_arms_of_the_United_Kingdom_(1952%E2%80%932022,_variant_1).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The European Convention on Human Rights was drafted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, under the oversight of Sir David Maxwell-Fyfe, who chaired the committee on legal and administrative questions of the council's Consultative Assembly from 1949 to 1952. As a founding member of the council, the United Kingdom acceded to the convention in March 1951, but British citizens could bring claims before the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg only from the 1960s.</p><p>During the 1980s groups such as Charter 88, which invoked the 300th anniversary of the Glorious Revolution and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a>, argued that a new British Bill of Rights was needed. The Labour Party pledged in its manifesto for the 1997 general election to incorporate the convention, and after its landslide victory the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tony-blair">Tony Blair</a> set out its plan in the White Paper Rights Brought Home. The act received royal assent on 9 November 1998.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Section 3 requires courts to read past and future legislation, so far as possible, in a way compatible with Convention rights, by reading words in, reading them out or choosing a compliant meaning. Where that cannot be done, sections 4 and 10 let higher courts issue a declaration of incompatibility, which does not invalidate the law but allows a minister to amend it by a remedial order approved by resolutions of each House of Parliament.</p><p>Section 6 counts courts and tribunals among public authorities, so the act also reaches disputes between private citizens. Section 19 requires a minister introducing a bill to state whether it is compatible. A late amendment, section 21(5), abolished the death penalty for the military offences that still carried it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first case invoking the act was brought by The Times in October 2000. In 2008 Paul Dacre, editor of the Daily Mail, criticised it for creating a right to privacy that Parliament had never passed. Constitutional critics argued that it gave unelected judges power over government policy, and the scholar Paul Craig held that it led courts to strained readings of statutes. Other critics said it hindered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of foreign criminals and terrorism suspects.</p><p>In 2005 the Conservatives under Michael Howard promised to overhaul or scrap the act. In 2007 his successor David Cameron vowed to replace it with a Bill of Rights for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and in 2014 the Conservative Party planned its repeal. Section 3 does not apply to the Illegal Migration Act 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Human Rights Act 1998” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Human Rights Act</b> of 1998 and the creation of a Supreme Court in 2009 added new judicial checks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
